WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.649
[Gentle music]
This is Storefront, a C# app that's running right now. SharpLsp attaches to it, no restart needed, and samples where its time goes.

00:13.383 --> 00:22.096
Stop the profiler, and every hot line lights up in the editor: the catalog parse, the discount loop, and the invoice built one string at a time.

00:23.450 --> 00:28.724
The results list the hottest functions and the hottest lines, so you know exactly where to start.

00:32.516 --> 00:38.238
Now track memory on the same app. SharpLsp snapshots the heap every ten seconds.

00:45.362 --> 00:55.168
The suspects rank by consistent growth, and CustomerSession grows in every snapshot, along with the byte arrays it holds. Click it, and you jump to its code.

00:58.395 --> 01:00.672
So, what's keeping those sessions alive?

01:16.304 --> 01:21.379
A static dictionary, named in the graph, that holds every session the app has ever seen.

01:25.304 --> 01:28.873
The fix: keep the last thousand orders, and evict the rest.

01:49.237 --> 01:51.750
Rebuild, run it again, and track memory.

02:05.429 --> 02:13.357
This time the heap holds steady. That's the SharpLsp profiler, for C# and F#. Free, and open source.
